      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-337211",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}-3{y}^{\u2032}+2y={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{\u2032}{y}_{1}^{\u2032}+{u}_{2}^{\u2032}{y}_{2}^{\u2032}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{\u2032}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{\u2032}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$"
